    "36624": {
        "order_id": 11,
        "exam_type": "WAEC",
        "subject": "Chemistry",
        "topic": "stoichiometry",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "1998",
        "question_text": "<p>Which of the following can be added to dissolve the precipitate formed in the reaction represented by the following equation? CaCl2(aq) + H2C2O 4(s) \u21cc CaC 2O2</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p><strong>Option B is the correct answer.&nbsp;</strong>The white precipitate formed in the given equilibrium reaction is calcium oxalate (CaC\\(_2\\)O\\(_4\\)). Calcium oxalate is the salt of a weak acid (ethanedioic acid, (H\\(_2\\)C\\(_2\\)O\\(_4\\)). When a strong acid like concentrated hydrochloric acid is added, it provides a high concentration of hydrogen ions (H\\(^+\\)). These (H\\(^+\\) ions react with the oxalate ions (C\\(_2\\)O\\(_4^{2-}\\)) to form poorly dissociated weak ethanedioic acid molecules. This removes free oxalate ions from the right side of the equilibrium, shifting the system back to the left according to Le Chatelier&#39;s principle<!--TgQPHd||[]-->, thereby dissolving the solid precipitate.<!--TgQPHd||[]-->&nbsp;</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 8,
